WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.640 --> 00:00:06.129
The details may be different, but make no
mistake, Brightburn is a reimagining of Superman’s

00:00:06.129 --> 00:00:10.270
origin story, asking what would’ve happened
to Clark Kent if the emergence of his powers

00:00:10.270 --> 00:00:11.780
made him lose his marbles.

00:00:11.780 --> 00:00:15.280
The answer is a lot of carnage, with the promise
of more to come.

00:00:15.580 --> 00:00:19.940
In the newer DC movies, Superman is known
to say that the S symbol on his chest stands

00:00:19.940 --> 00:00:20.600
for "hope”.

00:00:21.240 --> 00:00:25.340
That detail makes it quite chilling to consider
the possible meanings of the symbol Brandon

00:00:25.340 --> 00:00:27.100
leaves at the scene of his crimes.

00:00:27.680 --> 00:00:32.160
Just like Superman’s "S" most obviously
stands for "Superman," Brandon’s symbol

00:00:32.160 --> 00:00:34.840
seems to be his initials: "Brandon Breyer."

00:00:35.100 --> 00:00:40.200
It could also stand for the town's name, "Brightburn,"
which eventually becomes the superhero-like

00:00:40.200 --> 00:00:41.860
name Brandon is known by.

00:00:41.980 --> 00:00:45.000
But the symbol also serves two less obvious
purposes.

00:00:45.200 --> 00:00:49.880
First, it hints that Brandon's instability
isn't necessarily as new to him as the emergence

00:00:49.880 --> 00:00:51.000
of his super powers.

00:00:51.309 --> 00:00:56.380
We see the drawing scrawled all over his notebook,
suggesting he's been fixating on this ominous

00:00:56.380 --> 00:00:57.860
symbol for a long time.

00:00:58.480 --> 00:01:03.100
Second, it acts as a tribute to the old comic
book trope of giving characters first and

00:01:03.109 --> 00:01:07.540
last names starting with the same letter,
or in the case of Clark Kent, the same consonant

00:01:07.540 --> 00:01:12.120
sound: characters like Lois Lane, Lex Luthor,
Peter Parker, and so on.

00:01:12.560 --> 00:01:14.120
"Those are really good alliterations."

00:01:14.280 --> 00:01:16.020
"I don’t want to talk alliterations!"

00:01:16.540 --> 00:01:21.200
You know Brandon is entering his instant kill
mode when he slips a creepy face mask over

00:01:21.200 --> 00:01:21.700
his head.

00:01:22.060 --> 00:01:26.540
You might wonder why Brandon does that, since
mask-wearing is one of the few superhero tropes

00:01:26.540 --> 00:01:28.380
you can't trace back to Superman.

00:01:28.560 --> 00:01:30.280
He’s always been more of a glasses guy.

00:01:30.480 --> 00:01:32.100
"See he doesn’t really want to hurt anybody."

00:01:32.220 --> 00:01:32.720
"Uh-huhhh."

00:01:33.600 --> 00:01:37.620
While Brandon does take some precautions to
conceal his identity, that eventually seems

00:01:37.620 --> 00:01:39.080
to have little to do with the mask.

00:01:39.650 --> 00:01:43.920
When he attacks his family members, Brandon
dons the mask even though it's clear his victims

00:01:43.920 --> 00:01:45.100
know who he is.

00:01:45.360 --> 00:01:46.380
So why does he wear it?

00:01:46.540 --> 00:01:48.680
Firstly, it's creepy and looks cool.

00:01:49.080 --> 00:01:53.880
Second, Superman didn't wear a mask, and that
may be the point, setting Brandon visually

00:01:53.880 --> 00:01:55.300
apart from the Man of Steel.

00:01:55.520 --> 00:01:58.860
Most importantly, it sets Brandon apart from
us.

00:01:59.000 --> 00:02:02.960
One of the most telling moments in Brightburn
comes when Brandon tells his aunt Merilee

00:02:02.960 --> 00:02:04.420
that he's "superior."

00:02:04.940 --> 00:02:09.149
His mask is his way of telling his victims
that even though he doesn't look like an alien,

00:02:09.149 --> 00:02:12.020
he is something different, and they should
be afraid.

00:02:12.580 --> 00:02:16.140
Why Brandon ends the life of his father Kyle
isn't much of a mystery.

00:02:16.140 --> 00:02:20.810
In a misguided effort to stop his son before
he can kill again, Kyle shoots Brandon in

00:02:20.810 --> 00:02:23.780
the back of the head while the boy is distracted
on a hunting trip.

00:02:24.060 --> 00:02:28.560
Unfortunately, Brandon is as bulletproof as
his Kryptonian inspiration, and there’s

00:02:28.560 --> 00:02:30.020
no coming back from that betrayal.

00:02:30.510 --> 00:02:33.200
The death of Brandon's mother Tori is different.

00:02:33.200 --> 00:02:35.520
What's most telling is the way Brandon kills
her.

00:02:35.860 --> 00:02:40.360
After she attempts to stab him with a shard
of metal from his spaceship, he carries her

00:02:40.360 --> 00:02:44.580
above the clouds and lets go, allowing her
to fall to the ground and die.

00:02:44.920 --> 00:02:49.340
Throughout Brightburn, Tori defends Brandon
in spite of mounting evidence of his crimes.

00:02:49.500 --> 00:02:53.520
Even before she tries to kill him with a weapon
she knows he’s vulnerable to, her words

00:02:53.520 --> 00:02:55.400
of love and trust in him ring true.

00:02:55.880 --> 00:02:58.760
Tori is the last human Brandon has any faith
in.

00:02:59.200 --> 00:03:03.540
Even though he knows he’s literally a different
species, on some level he wants Tori to be

00:03:03.540 --> 00:03:06.440
like him, perhaps so he won’t be so alone.

00:03:06.680 --> 00:03:10.860
When he flies her into the sky and lets go,
it can almost be seen as a kind of a test.

00:03:10.860 --> 00:03:15.020
Maybe he doesn’t want her to fall, maybe
he wants her to fly alongside him, proving

00:03:15.020 --> 00:03:18.320
once and for all that she truly is his "real"
mother.

00:03:18.320 --> 00:03:23.120
When she predictably plummets to her death,
it's almost like a final confirmation to Brandon

00:03:23.120 --> 00:03:25.060
of how deeply alone he is.

00:03:25.500 --> 00:03:30.280
Right after killing his mother, Brandon causes
a passenger jet to crash down on the Breyer

00:03:30.280 --> 00:03:32.040
residence, killing everyone on board.

00:03:32.280 --> 00:03:35.000
We never see exactly what he does in his attack.

00:03:35.300 --> 00:03:39.880
First we see the jet heading toward him, and
then we cut to the next morning, when FBI

00:03:39.880 --> 00:03:41.540
agents are picking through the wreckage.

00:03:41.760 --> 00:03:46.360
Brandon is eating a cookie while resting in
the back of an ambulance, looking as innocent

00:03:46.360 --> 00:03:46.860
as can be.

00:03:47.620 --> 00:03:51.560
The most practical reason for Brandon destroying
the plane is to hide his crimes.

00:03:51.560 --> 00:03:56.920
But there's also a more symbolic reason, Brightburn
begins with a crash and ends with a crash.

00:03:57.180 --> 00:04:01.540
When Brandon's spaceship crashes on the Breyer
property in the beginning of the film, a kindly

00:04:01.540 --> 00:04:04.300
couple saves his life and raises him as their
own.

00:04:04.620 --> 00:04:09.740
Years later, Brandon causes a crash on the
same property; not only does he not save anyone,

00:04:10.020 --> 00:04:11.520
he makes sure everyone dies.

00:04:11.740 --> 00:04:15.220
It’s a message to the world that the mercy
his parents showed him when he was a baby

00:04:15.220 --> 00:04:16.880
will not be reciprocated.

00:04:17.180 --> 00:04:21.760
Brightburn ends with news reports from the
not-too-distant future showing that Brandon's

00:04:21.760 --> 00:04:23.500
not done with his path of destruction.

00:04:23.880 --> 00:04:28.260
We see huge buildings falling from his assaults,
as the news media begins referring to the

00:04:28.260 --> 00:04:32.800
strange flying phenomenon over Brightburn
by the shortened name of simply "Brightburn."

00:04:33.120 --> 00:04:37.720
But while the montage starts with more traditional
media outlets, it ends on the loony fringes,

00:04:37.720 --> 00:04:42.750
focusing on a video broadcast from a conspiracy
theorist played by Michael Rooker called the

00:04:42.750 --> 00:04:48.430
Big T. The program shows footage of Brightburn's
destruction and urges viewers to wake up and

00:04:48.430 --> 00:04:49.700
take the threat seriously.

00:04:50.260 --> 00:04:52.600
But Brightburn isn't the only threat he mentions.

00:04:53.040 --> 00:04:57.390
The broadcaster also makes reference to a
half-man, half-fish entity that’s reportedly

00:04:57.390 --> 00:04:59.720
taking out boats in the South China Sea.

00:05:00.060 --> 00:05:04.120
He also tells us about a so-called "witch
woman" who's supposedly strangling people

00:05:04.120 --> 00:05:05.440
to death with her rope.

00:05:05.780 --> 00:05:10.020
After what we’ve seen at this point, it’s
clear that these aren't Big T's fantasies.

00:05:10.360 --> 00:05:14.920
If he kept going we'd probably hear about
a half-man, half-bat and a man who moves so

00:05:14.920 --> 00:05:16.580
fast no one can see him.

00:05:17.160 --> 00:05:21.530
While this whole sequence could be a setup
for a sequel about a veritable Legion of Real-Life

00:05:21.530 --> 00:05:25.380
Doom, it also works as a nod to the comics
that inspired the film.

00:05:25.580 --> 00:05:30.680
More than anything, it helps to seal in Brightburn’s
biggest message, that in a different light,

00:05:30.680 --> 00:05:33.860
our greatest heroes can also be our worst
nightmares.

